      ],
      "text": "In mathematics, the quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) can be solved using the formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where the discriminant \\( \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \\) determines the nature of the roots. When \\( \\Delta > 0 \\), there are two distinct real roots; when \\( \\Delta = 0 \\), there is exactly one real root; and when \\( \\Delta < 0 \\), the roots are complex conjugates."
    },
    {
      "page": 2,
      "type": "text",
      "bbox": [
        213,
        625,
        784,
        709
      ],
      "text": "For sequences, the arithmetic series \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i\\) can be expressed as \\(\\frac{n(a_1 + a_n)}{2}\\), while the geometric series \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} ar^k\\) converges to \\(\\frac{a}{1-r}\\) when \\(|r| < 1\\). In calculus, the derivative \\(f'(x) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\\) represents the instantaneous rate of change, and the definite integral \\(\\int_a^b f(x) dx\\) gives the accumulated area under the curve."
